From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The Playport authors <dev@playport.dev>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 10:48:44 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] FEXCore: on iOS, decode no further than a call in writable
 code

PLA-873. Borderlands 2's launcher (Wine Mono, i386 WPF) called 0xfeec7cc0
from Mono's JIT code in a window procedure under ShowWindow. The address is
never mapped. It is what Mono's unpatched `call 0` placeholder becomes: the
call is emitted into a temporary buffer (here near 0x14a4ab50, in the
launcher's heap), copied to the code chunk, and patched in place
afterwards. The thread ran a translation of the call made before the patch.

Mono ends each method with its exception throws, calls to a throw helper
that never returns, and writes the next method right after it. Without
MonoHacks the multiblock decoder goes on past every call, so a block of the
earlier method translates the next one too, possibly between its memcpy and
its patches. A compile traps a code page only after decoding it. When the
patch lands in between, it does not fault, and the translation stays. In
the 140 log lines before the call, threads 0068 and 0088 both write Mono
code into that host page while blocks are compiled from it.

Upstream stops past calls only once Mono is detected by its DLL's name.
Wine Mono's detection is opt-in (MADEIRA_WINEMONO_BRIDGE), and in the WOW64
module every image's name is empty (GetSectionFilePath fails), so no 32-bit
Mono is ever detected. On iOS, DecodesPastCall (IosMultiblock.h) stops past
a call in every writable executable range, a JIT's code; an image's code
is decoded as before. The call's return site starts its own block, as it
does for Mono under MonoHacks.

Class: upstream-bug
Evidence: tools/tests/test_fex_decode_past_call.py (host check; the phone gate, Borderlands 2's launcher window over several launches, not run)
Offered-upstream: no; upstream's MonoHacks cover the same code where Mono is detected
---
 FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/Frontend.cpp    |  5 ++--
 FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/IosMultiblock.h | 25 +++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 28 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)
 create mode 100644 FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/IosMultiblock.h

diff --git a/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/Frontend.cpp b/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/Frontend.cpp
index e71850288..bfdf03942 100644
--- a/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/Frontend.cpp
+++ b/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/Frontend.cpp
@@ -10,6 +10,7 @@ $end_info$
 #include "Interface/Core/Frontend.h"
 #include "Interface/Core/OpcodeDispatcher.h"
 #include "Interface/Core/X86Tables/X86Tables.h"
+#include "Interface/Core/IosMultiblock.h"
 #include "Interface/Core/LookupCache.h"
 
 #include <array>
@@ -1136,10 +1137,10 @@ void Decoder::BranchTargetInMultiblockRange() {
   const auto InstEnd = DecodeInst->PC + DecodeInst->InstSize;
 
   if (DecodeInst->TableInfo->Flags & FEXCore::X86Tables::InstFlags::FLAGS_CALL) {
-    if (ExecutableRangeWritable && CTX->AreMonoHacksActive()) {
+    if (!DecodesPastCall(ExecutableRangeWritable, CTX->AreMonoHacksActive())) {
       // Mono generated code often contains noreturn calls with garbage following them, and calls are always backpatched
       // after CIL compilation leading to n recompiles for a multiblock with n calls. Choose to minimize stutters over
-      // raw performance and disable tracking past calls for mono generated code.
+      // raw performance and disable tracking past calls for mono generated code (on iOS, all writable code: IosMultiblock.h).
       return;
     }
 
diff --git a/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/IosMultiblock.h b/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/IosMultiblock.h
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..0e3bfef83
--- /dev/null
+++ b/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/IosMultiblock.h
@@ -0,0 +1,25 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
+#pragma once
+
+namespace FEXCore::Frontend {
+
+// Playport (PLA-873): whether the multiblock decoder goes on past a CALL, decoding the bytes after
+// it as the return site. Mono ends a method with its exception throws, each a call to a throw
+// helper that never returns, and writes the next method right after it: a memcpy, then every call
+// patched in place. Past that last call the decoder translates the next method, possibly between
+// its memcpy and its patches. A patch that lands before the compile traps the page (another
+// thread's compile; the trap comes after decoding) leaves that translation in place, and the
+// thread runs an unpatched `call 0` placeholder: Borderlands 2's launcher called 0xfeec7cc0 from
+// Wine Mono's code. Upstream stops only once Mono is detected by its DLL's name, which on iOS a
+// WoW64 module never has, and Wine Mono's detection is opt-in. On iOS no writable executable range
+// (a JIT's code) is decoded past a call; an image's code still is.
+inline bool DecodesPastCall(bool RangeWritable, bool MonoHacksActive) {
+#ifdef FEX_IOS_HOST
+  (void)MonoHacksActive;
+  return !RangeWritable;
+#else
+  return !(RangeWritable && MonoHacksActive);
+#endif
+}
+
+} // namespace FEXCore::Frontend
